   CONFIRMED DEFECT: `TestInflatedBracketTopForYear_GrowsWithCalendarYear`
   in analysis/tax_optimizer_strategies_test.go (diff hunk, lines ~184-198)
   computes its "independent" expected value for year 10 via:
     tc := engine.NewTaxCalculator(s.TaxConfig, s.InflationRate)
     wantY10, ok := bracketTopForRate(tc.GetAdjustedBrackets(engine.YearsFromTaxBase(s, 10)), 0.24)
   which is line-for-line the same call sequence as the implementation
   under test:
     func inflatedBracketTopForYear(...) (float64, bool) {
         tc := engine.NewTaxCalculator(s.TaxConfig, s.InflationRate)
         return bracketTopForRate(tc.GetAdjustedBrackets(engine.YearsFromTaxBase(s, projectionYear)), target)
     }
   Proved empirically in a throwaway copy: injecting a deliberate 5%
   mis-scale into `engine.GetAdjustedBrackets` (a real, plausible
   regression in the shared function this task consolidated onto) leaves
   `TestInflatedBracketTopForYear_GrowsWithCalendarYear` PASSING
   (`go test -run TestInflatedBracketTopForYear_GrowsWithCalendarYear -v`
   → PASS) while `internal/services/retirement/engine` tests correctly
   catch the same injected bug via TestVersionedConstants_PreserveExistingValues
   (independently derived from StandardDeduction2024/TaxBrackets2024
   literals). So the analysis-package test no longer protects against a
   computation regression in the exact function this task rewrote to
   consolidate the optimizer onto engine.GetAdjustedBrackets — the task's
   own stated purpose — even though the system as a whole still catches
   such a bug via the engine package's independent test. This is squarely
   the pattern the task brief named as a hunt target ("check whether any
   recalibrated test now merely asserts what the code emits (tautology)
   instead of an independently derived value"), it is inside this task's
   diff (analysis/tax_optimizer_strategies_test.go is in the manifest), and
   the remedy is narrow and cheap: recompute the year-10 expected ceiling
   from an independently-derived figure (e.g. the literal 2026 MFJ 24%
   bracket top compounded by the known inflation factor for the number of
   years past 2026) rather than by re-invoking GetAdjustedBrackets.

